# Last-handled neat-core version.
#
# Ockham consumes neat-core via an unpinned `path` dependency that tracks head.
# CI (`scripts/check-neat-core-version.sh`) fails when the sibling neat-core
# presents a breaking bump above this baseline — pre-1.0 a higher minor, or any
# higher major — forcing a deliberate upgrade. Clear the gate by updating
# `ockham` for the breaking change and bumping this version to match neat-core.
#
# 0.10.6: bootstrap against the current family pin (NEAT-AI-core #577 synapse
# uniqueness by (fromUUID, toUUID, type)). Ockham issue #1 does not yet mutate
# creatures; the pin documents the sibling we compile against.
#
# 0.10.6 -> 0.11.4: acknowledge neat-core #619 (0.11.0), the Pruning milestone
# roll-up (Issues #588, #589 and their siblings). It is additive: it adds the
# `prune_cleanup`, `prune_fixtures`, `prune_json`, `prune_neuron` and
# `prune_synapse` modules and their re-exports, and removes or renames nothing
# Ockham names (`CreatureExport`, `NeuronExport`, `SynapseExport`, `SquashType`,
# `compile_creature`, `apply_squash`, `parse_creature_json`, `creature_to_json*`,
# `parse_squash_name`, `parse_synapse_type`, `validate_no_duplicate_synapses`,
# `training_data`). The minor bump is the milestone policy in neat-core's
# RELEASING.md, not a signature change. Within the line, 0.11.2 (#626, Issue
# #613) withdrew the `weighted_sum_*_simd` kernels whose `get_unchecked`
# preconditions were caller-upheld; Ockham calls none of them. 0.11.1, 0.11.3
# and 0.11.4 are bump-deps.sh fixes with no library change. No Ockham source
# change was required; as Issue #183 reports, `cargo build`, clippy with `-D warnings`
# and the full test suite were green against 0.11.1 with no edit.
#
# 0.11.4 -> 0.13.0: acknowledge neat-core #633 (Issue #625, 0.12.0), which made
# every `CompiledNetwork` field private behind borrow-only accessors so safe
# code can no longer edit a validated network into an out-of-bounds SIMD read,
# and 0.13.0 (#637, the security-scan milestone roll-up; nothing Ockham names
# changed). Ockham read `num_inputs` and `activations` directly in `stats.rs`;
# those reads now go through `num_inputs()` and `activations()`. Unhandled, the
# same bump took the GRQ fleet down through rust_scorer (GRQ#4724); Ockham was
# next in line because GRQ builds it from the same sibling neat-core at head.
# Verified by `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` and the full test
# suite against the sibling clone at 0.13.0 (59eb6e6).

# 0.13.0 -> 0.14.1: acknowledge neat-core #640 (Issue #622, 0.14.0), which bounds
# a creature's DECLARED observation width before it is walked:
# `validate_creature_width` now refuses a declared `input` above MAX_NODE_COUNT
# with `CreatureError::TooManyNodes`, so `compile_creature`,
# `validate_creature_topology` and `cleanup_creature_with` no longer size an
# allocation by an unbounded literal. Pre-1.0 that is a minor bump because input
# previously accepted is now refused; 0.14.1 (#641) is a bump-deps.sh fix with no
# library change. Ockham names none of the affected items (no `TooManyNodes`,
# `validate_creature_width` or MAX_NODE_COUNT reference in `ockham/src`), and the
# creatures it screens are real exports far below the ceiling, so no Ockham
# source change was required. Verified against the sibling clone at Develop
# 0.14.1 (255b06e): `cargo build`, `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets
# --all-features -D warnings` and `cargo test --workspace --all-features`
# (665 lib + 56 integration tests) are all green.

# 0.14.1 -> 0.14.4: patch releases with no library change Ockham names. The
# sibling clone this branch builds against is 0.14.4, and Issue #182 compiles
# against its `prune_neuron` / `prune_synapse` surface (neat-core #587's
# Pruning milestone), so the recorded baseline moves with it.

# 0.14.4 -> 0.15.4: acknowledge neat-core #653 (Issue #601, 0.15.0), which adds
# the `NetworkError::InvalidInputCount { num_inputs, num_neurons }` variant.
# `CompiledNetwork::new` computed `num_neurons - num_inputs` straight from the
# 8-byte header, so a malformed buffer declaring more inputs than nodes wrapped
# that usize subtraction (release builds carry Cargo's default
# `overflow-checks = false`) and aborted the module in `Vec::with_capacity`; the
# header is now validated first and such a buffer returns the new variant.
# Pre-1.0 that is a breaking bump only because `NetworkError` is not
# `#[non_exhaustive]`, so a downstream exhaustive `match` must gain an arm.
# Ockham names `NetworkError` nowhere (no reference in `ockham/src` or
# `ockham/tests`): it reaches a network only through `compile_creature`, never
# through `CompiledNetwork::new` on a serialised buffer, and renders the error it
# does see with `to_string()` rather than matching it. No Ockham source change
# was required. 0.14.5-0.14.7 and 0.15.1-0.15.4 are patch releases
# (a CI exit-status fix, `MemeticExport::prune_to` bounding the declared
# observation width, a `propagate_codec` overflow fix, and bats/Deno workflow
# work) with no library change Ockham names. Verified against the Develop head
# clone at 0.15.4 (ed3165a): `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets
# --all-features -- -D warnings` and `cargo test --workspace --all-features`
# (675 lib + 70 integration tests) are green with no edit.

# 0.15.4 -> 0.16.0: acknowledge Ockham #196 (neat-core 0.16.0), which widens the
# pruning compensation rule. `prune_neuron` and `prune_synapse` used to refuse a
# bias fold to **every** aggregate target and name it on
# `PruneResult::uncompensated`; a target the cut leaves with **no inward edge**
# now takes the fold, because the forward pass evaluates a zero-edge MINIMUM,
# MAXIMUM, MEAN or HYPOT from its bias alone. HYPOT folds the magnitude `|W.mu|`,
# and a bare HYPOTv2 target — whose bias lives inside a per-synapse square that
# no longer exists — is rewritten to ABSOLUTE over the folded bias. `IF` is
# excluded: what it lost is a role, and IfRepair owns that. Pre-1.0 that is a
# minor bump because documented pruning behaviour callers rely on changed, not
# because any public item did: no signature, type or name Ockham compiles
# against moved. Ockham only *reports* what a `PruneResult` carries
# (`ockham/src/prune.rs::PruneDetail::absorb` copies `bias_folds`,
# `uncompensated`, `transform` and the rest into its journal records), so what
# it sees is a better-compensated candidate — fewer `aggregate-target` entries,
# and a `TransformClass::Exact` where the source's value was fixed — with no
# Ockham source change required. Verified against the sibling clone at 0.16.0
# (bc80140): `cargo test --workspace --all-features` (675 lib + 70 integration
# tests) and `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features -- -D
# warnings` are green with no edit.

# Within the same 0.16.0 line, Ockham #198 makes `prune_neuron` ask cleanup for
# `IfRepair::Rewrite` — the exact `IF` repair `prune_synapse` already used —
# instead of the TypeScript-parity `IfRepair::Downgrade`. An `IF` a neuron
# removal leaves short of a role is now flattened onto the arm a statically
# decided condition always takes, or given back the emptied arm on a zero-weight
# support edge, so `PruneResult::downgraded_if_neurons` is always empty on both
# entry points and `static_if_neurons` / `restored_if_roles` carry what happened.
# `PruneResult::transform` also reaches `Exact` where the rewrite proves the lost
# term cost nothing. Pre-1.0 that is a minor bump because documented pruning
# behaviour callers rely on changed, not because any public item did. Ockham only
# *reports* what a `PruneResult` carries (`ockham/src/prune.rs::PruneDetail::absorb`),
# so what it sees is a better-rewritten candidate; the one edit is the doc comment
# on `PruneDetail::downgraded_if_neurons`, which now records that the list can no
# longer fill. Verified against the sibling clone on
# `ockham-198-prune-neuron-if-rewrite` (0.16.0): `./quality.sh` is green here,
# including `scripts/check-neat-core-version.sh` and the full suite.

# 0.16.0 -> 0.18.0: acknowledge Ockham #201 (neat-core 0.18.0). Two things reach
# Ockham, neither of them a source change here. First, the Ockham #198 entry is
# retitled to the version it actually shipped as, 0.17.0 — its branch was cut at
# 0.15.9 and claimed 0.16.0, #197 took that slot first, and core's auto-bump
# moved it to 0.17.0 on merge. The behaviour was already acknowledged above;
# only the number it carries was wrong. Second, 0.18.0 lands the Ockham #196
# zero-edge bias fold, acknowledged above at 0.16.0 and described exactly as it
# ships: its core branch was pushed but its PR was never opened, so the rule was
# never on Develop, and the #201 branch merges it because corner case (11) is
# that rule. The #201 work itself is test-only on the behaviour side — corner
# cases 1-12 driven through `prune_neuron_json` / `prune_synapse_json` and added
# to the golden parity record — plus one additive public item,
# `impl From<&PruneResult> for PruneResponse`, which was the private
# `PruneResponse::from_result`. Nothing Ockham compiles against was removed,
# renamed or narrowed, and Ockham calls the native entry points rather than the
# JSON ones. One behaviour moves with it, found by driving the shapes across
# the wire: the zero-edge `HYPOTv2 -> ABSOLUTE` rewrite is now named on
# `PruneResult::converted_neurons` like the single-edge conversion already was,
# where before it happened silently. Ockham reads that list nowhere in
# `ockham/src` — `PruneDetail::absorb` copies the fold, uncompensated and
# transform fields — so it sees a more complete report and needs no edit. Verified against the sibling clone on
# `ockham-201-prune-json-corner-cases` (0.18.0): `./quality.sh` is green here,
# including `scripts/check-neat-core-version.sh` and the full suite.

