# 0.18.0 -> 0.22.1: acknowledge Ockham #221, which covers four core minors at
# once — 0.19.0 (core #685/#686, Issue #682), 0.20.0 (#689, Issue #680), 0.21.0
# (#698) and 0.22.0 (#703, Issue #688) — and the 0.19.1, 0.21.1, 0.21.2 and
# 0.22.1 patches (a wasm-bindgen bump, two runlib.sh / family-pin script fixes
# and a stale `wasm-bench/Cargo.lock`), none of which touches `neat-core/src`.
# Two of the minors carry nothing Ockham can see: 0.20.0 made `scripts/runlib.sh`
# the canonical build-install-clean helper (adopted here by #222) and 0.21.0 is
# the Scan 20260911 milestone roll-up, both recorded in core's RELEASING.md as
# "no public-API or behaviour change". 0.19.0 adds
# `CreatureError::UnknownTargetUuid(String)` and makes `compile_creature` refuse
# a synapse whose `toUUID` names no listed neuron, where it used to drop the
# edge silently and compile a network one synapse smaller than declared.
# Pre-1.0 that is breaking twice over: the enum is not `#[non_exhaustive]`, so
# an exhaustive `match` must gain an arm, and input that compiled is now
# refused. Ockham names `CreatureError` nowhere (no reference in `ockham/src`
# or `ockham/tests`; it renders a compile error with `to_string()`), and every
# creature it prunes is a real export core validates, whose every `toUUID` is
# a listed neuron, so a refusal names a defect that was already scored wrong.
# No Ockham source change for it. 0.22.0 is the one that reaches Ockham:
# `prune_neuron` and `prune_synapse` now ask cleanup to splice out a hidden
# `IDENTITY` pass-through — the shape the `IF` rewrite and the single-edge
# aggregate conversion both leave behind — wiring its sources straight into its
# targets at `w_in · w_out` and folding its bias into each target, and
# `CleanupOptions`, `CleanupOutcome`, `PruneResult` and `PruneResponse` each
# gain a field (`splice_identity` / `spliced_neurons`) with no
# `#[non_exhaustive]`. Ockham builds none of the four by struct literal and
# destructures none, so it compiles unchanged; what it sees is a candidate that
# comes back without any hidden `IDENTITY` relay it carried — not only the one
# the conversion made (`single_edge_minimum_target` now returns with no `h_min`
# at all, its `convertedNeurons` row still naming the rewrite that preceded the
# splice) but every relay elsewhere in the creature, since the cleanup walks
# the whole creature on every request. Every splice computes the same number,
# so `TransformClass` does not move and no candidate becomes approximate for
# it. In a run this changes little: the exact cleanup pre-pass
# (`ockham/src/canonical.rs`, `identity-collapse`) already retires every hidden
# `IDENTITY` before the sweep starts, so core only meets one under
# `--no-exact-cleanup`. Two Ockham edits follow. First, `PruneDetail::absorb`
# now copies `PruneResult::spliced_neurons` onto `PruneDetail::spliced_neurons`
# (`splicedNeurons`), so a run's journal names which neurons the splice retired
# instead of leaving a reader to reconcile a neuron count against the requested
# and cascade lists alone. Second, thirteen unit tests in `prune.rs`, `sweep.rs`
# and `run.rs` used a hidden `IDENTITY` as a bystander that had to survive a
# neighbour's cut — `h_keep`, `h_b`, the parallel relays of `hidden_paths` —
# and the splice took it; those bystanders are now `TANH`, through
# squash-parameterised fixtures, so each test still proves the cascade, fold,
# group and bundle bookkeeping it was written for, while the `IDENTITY`
# defaults stay for the tests about Ockham's own collapse rung. Verified
# against the sibling clone at Develop 0.22.1 (4a10d7b): every check
# `./quality.sh` runs is green here — `scripts/check-neat-core-version.sh`,
# codespell, markdownlint, actionlint, cargo-deny, `cargo fmt --check`, clippy
# with `-D warnings`, rustdoc with `-D warnings` and the full suite (704 lib +
# 73 integration tests).

